---
import MarketingLayout from "../layouts/MarketingLayout.astro";
---

<MarketingLayout
  title="The human review layer for AI agents · Gatewerk"
  description="A review layer is the piece of infrastructure where agent actions stop and wait for a person: one inbox, one record, any stack."
>

  <!-- ─── ambient glow ─── -->
  <div style="position:absolute;top:-260px;left:50%;transform:translateX(-50%);width:1100px;height:600px;background:radial-gradient(ellipse at center,rgba(33,181,113,.1),transparent 68%);pointer-events:none;filter:blur(20px);z-index:0;"></div>

  <section style="position:relative;z-index:1;max-width:820px;margin:0 auto;padding:84px 32px 96px;">

    <h1 class="gw-up gw-display" style="font-weight:600;font-size:clamp(34px,5vw,48px);line-height:1.06;letter-spacing:-.03em;color:#f7f7f1;margin:0;">The human review layer for AI agents</h1>

    <p class="gw-up" style="max-width:640px;margin:22px 0 0;font-size:17px;line-height:1.6;color:#b4b4ac;text-wrap:pretty;">
      Your agents act in the world. They send the email, publish the post,
      move the money, ship the code. A review layer is the piece of
      infrastructure where those actions stop and wait for a person: one inbox
      where a human reviews the exact payload, edits it, and decides, and one
      record that proves someone looked.
    </p>
    <p class="gw-up" style="max-width:640px;margin:12px 0 0;font-size:15px;line-height:1.65;color:#9a9a8f;text-wrap:pretty;">
      This page is the standing definition of that layer. It is kept current
      as Gatewerk changes.
    </p>

    <h2 class="gw-display" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,28px);font-weight:600;letter-spacing:-.02em;color:#f7f7f1;margin:56px 0 0;">Why a layer, and not a feature</h2>
    <p style="margin:20px 0 0;font-size:15px;line-height:1.65;color:#9a9a8f;">
      Most agent frameworks can pause. LangGraph has an interrupt, n8n has a
      wait node, and every team has built a Slack button once. Each of those
      pauses one workflow inside one system, and each keeps its own partial
      log, or none.
    </p>
    <p style="margin:16px 0 0;font-size:15px;line-height:1.65;color:#e4e0d6;">
      An interrupt pauses one workflow inside one framework. Gatewerk is the
      review layer across all of them: one inbox, one record, any stack.
    </p>
    <p style="margin:16px 0 0;font-size:15px;line-height:1.65;color:#9a9a8f;">
      That is the whole argument for a layer. The actions that need a human do
      not come from one framework, so the place they stop cannot live inside
      one framework. A review layer sits between your agents and the world,
      speaks to every stack the same way, and writes every decision into the
      same record.
    </p>

    <h2 class="gw-display" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,28px);font-weight:600;letter-spacing:-.02em;color:#f7f7f1;margin:56px 0 0;">The belief underneath</h2>
    <p style="margin:20px 0 0;font-size:17px;line-height:1.6;color:#e4e0d6;font-weight:600;">
      Work done for humans is decided by humans.
    </p>
    <p style="margin:16px 0 0;font-size:15px;line-height:1.65;color:#9a9a8f;">
      Agents keep getting more capable, and the work they carry keeps growing.
      We believe the main decisions in that work still belong to people. An
      agent can draft, execute, route, and carry. That is the operational
      load, and agents should take as much of it as you trust them with. The
      decision, and the ownership that comes with it, stays human. Human in
      the loop is not a safety feature bolted on. It is the point.
    </p>
    <p style="margin:16px 0 0;font-size:15px;line-height:1.65;color:#9a9a8f;">
      Three commitments follow.
    </p>
    <p style="margin:16px 0 0;font-size:15px;line-height:1.65;color:#9a9a8f;">
      <strong style="color:#f1f1ec;font-weight:600;">Framework and platform agnostic.</strong> The belief is about humans and
      decisions, not about any AI vendor or stack. Gatewerk works with every
      agent framework and depends on none. HRP is an open wire specification;
      the REST API, both SDKs, the MCP server, and the n8n nodes all land in
      the same inbox.
    </p>
    <p style="margin:16px 0 0;font-size:15px;line-height:1.65;color:#9a9a8f;">
      <strong style="color:#f1f1ec;font-weight:600;">Open source.</strong> An audit trail you cannot inspect is marketing. The
      record of your judgment lives on your infrastructure, under a license
      that keeps it open. Self hosting is free forever, and the open source
      version is never a crippled version of the cloud.
    </p>
    <p style="margin:16px 0 0;font-size:15px;line-height:1.65;color:#9a9a8f;">
      <strong style="color:#f1f1ec;font-weight:600;">Decision points designed by a human and decided by a human.</strong> You
      choose where judgment enters the pipeline: which actions gate, which
      act under a veto window, which run free. And you exercise the judgment. A human
      decides, never a model. Gatewerk will not ship a model that reviews your
      agents, because "a model checked it" is exactly the answer that does not
      hold up when someone asks who decided.
    </p>

    <h2 class="gw-display" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,28px);font-weight:600;letter-spacing:-.02em;color:#f7f7f1;margin:56px 0 0;">Humans move up, not out</h2>
    <p style="margin:20px 0 0;font-size:15px;line-height:1.65;color:#9a9a8f;">
      The role of the human climbs a ladder: deciding every instance, then
      designing the decision points, then curating policy over documented
      judgment. Autonomy is earned through the record. Blocking, monitoring,
      and auto approve are three positions on one dial, and the decision
      history is the evidence a human uses to move it: where agents get
      overridden, which templates get edited versus decided cleanly, how
      corrections trend.
    </p>
    <p style="margin:16px 0 0;font-size:15px;line-height:1.65;color:#9a9a8f;">
      This is why a review layer gets more valuable as agents improve, not
      less. Better agents produce more delegation, and more delegation needs
      more legible judgment.
    </p>

    <h2 class="gw-display" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,28px);font-weight:600;letter-spacing:-.02em;color:#f7f7f1;margin:56px 0 0;">What the layer provides</h2>
    <p style="margin:20px 0 0;font-size:15px;line-height:1.65;color:#9a9a8f;">
      <strong style="color:#f1f1ec;font-weight:600;">The record.</strong> Every decision lands in a tamper evident, HMAC chained
      audit log. It preserves every distinction of the process: approved,
      rejected, edited, vetoed, or confirmed; attributed to the specific human
      or explicitly to the system. A lapsed veto window is logged as the
      absence of an objection, never dressed up as human sign off.
    </p>
    <p style="margin:16px 0 0;font-size:15px;line-height:1.65;color:#9a9a8f;">
      <strong style="color:#f1f1ec;font-weight:600;">Edit, then decide.</strong> A review is not a yes/no ping. Reviewers see a
      typed form, correct the payload inline, and the agent receives the
      corrected value. What the agent suggested and what the human decided are
      both kept.
    </p>
    <p style="margin:16px 0 0;font-size:15px;line-height:1.65;color:#9a9a8f;">
      <strong style="color:#f1f1ec;font-weight:600;">Decisions by link, no account.</strong> A client, a vendor, or outside
      counsel can decide one review by share link, with no seat and no signup,
      on the same record.
    </p>
    <p style="margin:16px 0 0;font-size:15px;line-height:1.65;color:#9a9a8f;">
      <strong style="color:#f1f1ec;font-weight:600;">One inbox, one logbook, one MCP surface.</strong> Agents file reviews over
      REST, the SDKs, MCP, or n8n. Humans decide from the inbox, or from any
      chat that speaks MCP. The dashboard is one client of the system, not the
      system.
    </p>
    <p style="margin:16px 0 0;font-size:15px;line-height:1.65;color:#9a9a8f;">
      <strong style="color:#f1f1ec;font-weight:600;">The decision history is a dataset.</strong> Every decision stores what the
      agent suggested, what the human decided, and why. It is queryable and
      exportable, so the same log that proves the past improves the future.
    </p>
    <p style="margin:16px 0 0;font-size:15px;line-height:1.65;color:#9a9a8f;">
      <strong style="color:#f1f1ec;font-weight:600;">Monitoring mode.</strong> For reversible actions the agent proceeds and a
      human can veto within a window. Oversight without blocking, which is the
      honest answer to "reviews slow my agents down".
    </p>

    <h2 class="gw-display" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,28px);font-weight:600;letter-spacing:-.02em;color:#f7f7f1;margin:56px 0 0;">Where to start</h2>
    <p style="margin:20px 0 0;font-size:15px;line-height:1.65;color:#9a9a8f;">
      Gatewerk is open source and self hosted: one script, Docker, your
      server. The quickstart takes under ten minutes.
    </p>

    <div style="margin-top:32px;display:flex;gap:14px;flex-wrap:wrap;">
      <a
        href="/docs/quickstart"
        style="display:inline-flex;align-items:center;height:42px;padding:0 20px;border-radius:10px;border:1px solid rgba(240,240,220,.14);color:#e4e0d6;font-size:14px;font-weight:600;text-decoration:none;transition:background .15s,border-color .15s;"
        onmouseover="this.style.background='rgba(240,240,220,.05)';this.style.borderColor='rgba(240,240,220,.24)'"
        onmouseout="this.style.background='';this.style.borderColor='rgba(240,240,220,.14)'"
      >
        Get started
      </a>
      <a
        href="/docs/protocol/hrp"
        style="display:inline-flex;align-items:center;height:42px;padding:0 20px;border-radius:10px;border:1px solid rgba(240,240,220,.14);color:#e4e0d6;font-size:14px;font-weight:600;text-decoration:none;transition:background .15s,border-color .15s;"
        onmouseover="this.style.background='rgba(240,240,220,.05)';this.style.borderColor='rgba(240,240,220,.24)'"
        onmouseout="this.style.background='';this.style.borderColor='rgba(240,240,220,.14)'"
      >
        Read the protocol
      </a>
    </div>

  </section>

</MarketingLayout>
